For high-performance XML parsing in Raku, there are several libraries depending on whether you need a DOM-based approach (loading the whole tree) or a SAX/StAX-based approach (streaming for very large files).

Here are the primary recommendations:

### 1. XML::Parser (The Standard)
This is the most common library. It is versatile and supports various parsing modes. For speed, it is often used as a wrapper for underlying C libraries.

**Example:**
```raku
use XML::Parser;

my $parser = XML::Parser.new;
$parser.parse(my $content = '<root><item>Data</item></root>');
```

### 2. XML::LibXML (High Performance)
If speed is your primary concern, **XML::LibXML** is generally the fastest option because it binds to the highly optimized C library `libxml2`. It provides a high-performance DOM tree structure.

**Example:**
```raku
use XML::LibXML;

my $doc = XML::LibXML.parse(file => 'large_data.xml');
my $items = $doc->findnodes('//item');
for @$items -> $item {
    say $item->text;
}
```

### 3. XML::SAX (For Massive Files)
If you are dealing with XML files too large to fit into memory, a SAX (Simple API for XML) parser is the fastest way to process data because it streams the file and triggers events.

**Example:**
```raku
use XML::SAX;

my $handler = XML::SAX.handler(
    start-element => sub { 
        my ($tag) = @_; 
        say "Started: $tag" if $tag eq 'item';
    },
    characters    => sub { 
        my ($content) = @_; 
        # Process text here
    },
);

XML::SAX.parse(file => 'huge_file.xml', handler => $handler);
```

